    "body": "> This specifies existing webkit behavior but I'm not sure it's the desirable one. It means that when you `window.open()` a popup it starts as insecure, and then becomes \"secure\" once it navigates.\r\n> \r\n> This seems to have a history. See #6369, [w3c/webappsec-secure-contexts#27](https://github.com/w3c/webappsec-secure-contexts/issues/27).\r\n\r\nAfter some further investigation into this checking against chromium/firefox source to resolve my confusion, I now think I understand. The direction to specify that seems to mean exposing \"precursor\" origin. That does seem like a decently larger size specification change however! I wonder how this was never exposed before?\r\n\r\n> This specifies existing webkit behavior but I'm not sure it's the desirable one\r\n\r\nI suppose in some ways this PR does keep that behaviour the _same_ as it was previously, it just doesn't alter it to match chromium/firefox. So perhaps one other approach than tackling all of precursor origin might be to split that issue out? Or should it be tackled here also?",
