    "updated_at": "2026-11-15T12:27:43Z",
    "body": "\r\nOkay, with the help of AI and looking at adding some more WPT cases, here's a table (coloured to make it hopefully a bit easier to read) of what I believe the different behaviours are:\r\n\r\n| Case | Top-level page | With precursor | Spec today | #13110 as written | Chrome 157 | Firefox 160 | Safari 26.5 |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| Same-origin popup navigated to `about:blank` | http: | **false** | ❌ true | ✅ false | ✅ false | ✅ false | ✅ false |\r\n| Same-origin popup navigated to `about:blank` | https: | **true** | ✅ true | ✅ true | ✅ true | ✅ true | ✅ true |\r\n| Initial `about:blank` of a popup opened by a non-secure https: frame | http: | **true** | ❌ false | ✅ true | ✅ true | ✅ true | ✅ true |\r\n| …the same popup after loading a same-origin document | http: | **true** | ❌ false | ✅ true | ✅ true | ✅ true | ✅ true |\r\n| Initial `about:blank` of a popup opened by a sandboxed https: frame | http: | **true** | ❌ false | ❌ false | ✅ true | ✅ true | ❌ false |\r\n| Initial `about:blank` of a popup opened by a sandboxed srcdoc frame | http: | **false** | ✅ false | ✅ false | ✅ false | ✅ false | ✅ false |\r\n| Initial `about:blank` of a popup opened by a sandboxed data: frame | http: | **false** | ✅ false | ✅ false | ✅ false | ✅ false | ✅ false |\r\n| Initial `about:blank` of a popup opened by a sandboxed https: frame | https: | **true** | ✅ true | ❌ false | ✅ true | ✅ true | ❌ false |\r\n| Initial `about:blank` of a popup opened by a sandboxed srcdoc frame | https: | **true** | ✅ true | ❌ false | ✅ true | ✅ true | ❌ false |\r\n| Initial `about:blank` of a popup opened by a sandboxed data: frame | https: | **false** | ❌ true | ✅ false | ✅ false | ❌ true | ✅ false |\r\n\r\nThese tests are not extensive, there are some more I need to dig into (nested cases), but overall I think the Chromium behaviour is best.\r\n\r\nSo far, trying to look into other options I don't think there is a good option other than precursor to represent all of this behaviour cleanly. But it does have specification fallout. So I suppose my question is to @annevk about whether I should try and push forward looking into adding that spec definition since WebKit behaviour is the diverging case here.",
